outputs.exact_statement:
The only nonzero positive-weight transfer quotients are at weights $r=2^k$, $k\geq0$. As Steenrod modules they are $$\label{eq:weight-modules}
 M_0(n)=\Sigma^n\mathbb F_2,\qquad
 M_k(n)=\Sigma^n d_0^nD_k\quad(k\geq1),\qquad n\geq0.$$ For $n>0$ these give the weight decomposition of $QB_n$. The action on $d_0^nD_k$ is its ordinary action as an ideal in $P_k$.*


*The dual of <a href="#eq:weight-suspension" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:weight-suspension">[eq:weight-suspension]</a> on these quotients is, after the common grading suspension, the inclusion $$\label{eq:module-suspension}
 d_0^{n+1}D_k\hookrightarrow d_0^nD_k
 \qquad(k\geq1).$$ In weight one it is the identity with the corresponding shift.

There is an equivalence of augmented commutative ring spectra $$\label{eq:zero-localization}
 R:=\Sigma^\infty_+QS^0
   \simeq\mathbb P(S\{u\})[(1+u)^{-1}],\qquad t=1+u,$$ and compatible augmented commutative ring maps $$\label{eq:weight-projections}
 R\longrightarrow\mathbb P_{\leq N}(S\{u\}),\qquad N\geq0.$$ The induced homology maps jointly detect all classes. Consequently a nonzero positive-dimensional class has a well-defined smallest nonzero weight, called its *leading weight*. Multiplication by a power of $t$ does not change its leading component.*

*Let $z\in H_dQ_0S^0$, $d>0$, be primitive and annihilated by positive Steenrod operations. Its leading component $z_r$ is primitive and $A$-annihilated for the linear coproduct calculation of Lemma <a href="#lem:weight-modules" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:weight-modules">9</a> at $n=0$. In particular $r=2^k$ for some $k\geq1$. The weight-$r$ component of $\sigma_*z$ is the image of $z_r$ under <a href="#eq:weight-suspension" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:weight-suspension">[eq:weight-suspension]</a>; its functional on $M_k(1)$ is the restriction, shifted by one, of the functional on $D_k$ defined by $z_r$. If $d$ is odd, this component is nonzero.

Under these assumptions, the cohomology extension of the cone induces an extension of graded $A$-modules $$\label{eq:indecomposable-cone-extension}
0\longrightarrow\mathbb F_2\{a\}\longrightarrow\mathcal E
\longrightarrow Q B\longrightarrow0,$$ where $|a|=2m$. On any lift of a degree-$m$ class $v\in Q B$, the operation $\mathop{\mathrm{Sq}}^m$ has value $\lambda(v)a$.

For points $x,y\in QS^0$, there is a homotopy $$\label{eq:weight-product}
 j_i(x+y)\simeq j_i(x)+j_i(y)
       +\sum_{0<t<i}j_t(x)\circ j_{i-t}(y),$$ where addition in the target is its additive infinite loop structure. For positive primitives $A_1,A_2\in H_*Q_0S^0$, this implies $$\label{eq:primitive-weight-product}
 J_i(A_1*A_2)
 =(J_iA_1)*(J_iA_2)
   +\sum_{0<t<i}(J_tA_1)\circ(J_{i-t}A_2).$$ Each $J_i$ preserves primitivity and $A$-annihilation. In particular, $$\label{eq:weight-square}
 J_i(A^{*2})=(J_iA)^{*2}
 +\begin{cases}(J_{i/2}A)^{\circ2},&i\text{ even},\\0,&i\text{ odd}.
 \end{cases}$$

Let $E_1\wedge E_2\to E_3$ be a pairing of spectra. If $u\in H_a\Omega^\infty E_1$ is homogeneous and $y\in H_b\Omega^\infty E_2$, where $b>0$, is $A$-annihilated, then, for $i\geq a$, $$\label{eq:pairing-rule}
 (Q^i u)\circ y=
 \begin{cases}
 Q^i(u\circ y),&i\geq a+b,\\
 0,&a\leq i<a+b.
 \end{cases}$$ If, in addition, $y$ is primitive and belongs to the basepoint component, then $(u_1*u_2)\circ y=0$ for positive-dimensional $u_1,u_2$. Translating a positive-dimensional first factor by a component point does not change its pairing with $y$.

The above-floor part of $H_0(E,d_0^pD_k)$ is an $A/K$-submodule. After identifying $A/K$ with the Steenrod algebra by halving degrees, the map $$\label{eq:cartier}
 C:\quad d_0^c\prod_{j=1}^{k-1}d_j^{b_j}
 \longmapsto
 d_0^{(c+k-2)/2}\prod_{j=1}^{k-1}d_j^{(b_j-1)/2}$$ identifies it with an ideal in $D_k$. More precisely, if $c_0$ is the least integer greater than $p$ with $c_0\equiv k\pmod2$, the target ideal is $d_0^{(c_0+k-2)/2}D_k$. Internal polynomial degrees transform by $d\mapsto(d-k)/2$.

Let $n\geq0$ and $n\equiv k\pmod2$. On cycles of even total degree, the projection $$\label{eq:tor-injection}
 I\otimes_A M_k(n)\longrightarrow
 I_{A/K}\otimes_{A/K}H_0(E,M_k(n))$$ is injective.

The total Steenrod square on a Dickson generator is $$\label{eq:algebra-total-dickson-square}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Sq}}(d_j)=d_j^2+
       \left(\sum_{i=0}^jd_i\right)
       \left(\sum_{a=j+1}^kd_a\right).$$ In particular the operation one below the top on $d_j$ is $d_0d_{j+1}$. For a positive-degree monomial $v=\prod_{j=0}^{k-1}d_j^{b_j}$ of internal degree $d$, $$\label{eq:algebra-one-below-top}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Sq}}^{d-1}v
 =v^2\sum_{j:\ b_j\ \text{odd}}\frac{d_0d_{j+1}}{d_j^2}.$$ Every displayed summand is a polynomial.
